	<h2>1. American Origins</h2>
	<div class="content-content">


<figure class="wp-block-image alignwide size-full"><img fetchpriority="high" decoding="async" width="1000" height="381" src="https://www.americanyawp.com/textbook/wp-content/uploads/2026/05/cahokia_21-1000x3811-1.jpg" alt="This painting shows Cahokia as it may have appeared around 1150 CE. Wattle and daub homes in various stages of construction are seen in the foreground with Indigenous folks farming, drying hides and going about daily activities. In the background is the very large, pyramid shaped temple known as Monks Mound can been seen. The temple has several levels with steps to ascend. Painting by Michael Hampshire for the Cahokia Mounds State Historic Site." class="wp-image-56" srcset="https://www.americanyawp.com/textbook/wp-content/uploads/2026/05/cahokia_21-1000x3811-1.jpg 1000w, https://www.americanyawp.com/textbook/wp-content/uploads/2026/05/cahokia_21-1000x3811-1-300x114.jpg 300w, https://www.americanyawp.com/textbook/wp-content/uploads/2026/05/cahokia_21-1000x3811-1-768x293.jpg 768w" sizes="(max-width: 1000px) 100vw, 1000px" /><figcaption class="wp-element-caption">Figure 1.1. One of the most advanced and influential settlements of the 12th century, Cahokia was located across from the Mississippi River in present-day St. Louis. At that time, it possessed a population greater than London. Cahokia Mounds State Historic Site.</figcaption></figure>



